      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The most common arguments against high-density zoning and new developments in established neighborhoods are rooted in the notion that they'll lead to over-development, gentrification, and displacement of long-time residents. These concerns are often fueled by the misconception that developers are only interested in lining their pockets with profits, rather than contributing to the community's well-being. The \"not in my backyard\" mentality is a classic example of this, where residents fear that new developments will bring in noise, traffic, and decreased property values.",
